                {"heading": "Passwords and passphrases", "body": "A password should be difficult to guess and unique to its account. Reusing one password lets a breach at one service unlock other accounts. A longer passphrase made from several unrelated words can be easier to remember and harder to guess than a short, predictable pattern. Avoid personal facts, common substitutions, and passwords shared with other people. A reputable password manager can create and store unique credentials, protected by a strong master credential and device security."},
                {"heading": "Multi-factor authentication", "body": "Authentication checks that a claimant is the person or service they say they are. Multi-factor authentication (MFA) combines evidence from different categories: something you know, something you have, or something you are. Two passwords are still one factor category. MFA can reduce the impact of a stolen password, but approval prompts can be abused through repeated requests or convincing deception. Deny prompts you did not initiate and report repeated unexpected prompts."},
                {"heading": "Authentication versus authorization", "body": "Authentication answers “Who are you?” Authorization answers “What are you allowed to do?” After a user signs in, permissions should match their job and be limited to the resources and actions they need. A successful login is not proof that every requested action should be permitted. Systems should check authorization at each protected operation and record important administrative activity."},
                {"heading": "Account recovery and sessions", "body": "Recovery channels such as email addresses, phone numbers, recovery codes, and trusted devices are part of account security. Keep them current and protected. Sign out of shared devices, protect devices with screen locks, and do not share one-time codes. If a credential may be exposed, use a trusted device to change it, revoke active sessions when possible, and report the event. A password change alone may not terminate a stolen session."},
