                {"heading": "Finding and assessing weaknesses", "body": "A vulnerability is a weakness in software, configuration, process, or design. Vulnerability assessment identifies and prioritizes potential weaknesses using inventories, configuration checks, and authorized scanners. A scanner can produce false positives and miss context, so findings need validation. Penetration testing is a controlled assessment in which authorized specialists attempt to demonstrate how selected weaknesses could be combined or exploited within an agreed scope."},
                {"heading": "Authorization comes first", "body": "Security testing must have explicit permission, a defined target list, allowed methods, a schedule, contact points, and stop conditions. Testing a public system without authorization can disrupt services, expose private information, or violate law and policy. Never scan or probe systems just because they appear reachable. Practice on intentionally designed training environments and follow their rules. In RedLab, network and attack-path exercises evaluate a local graph; they do not send attacks to external hosts."},
                {"heading": "Prioritize remediation", "body": "A vulnerability score is only one input. Consider whether the affected asset is exposed, how difficult exploitation is, what data or service could be affected, whether protections already exist, and how quickly a fix can be applied safely. Fix internet-exposed critical weaknesses promptly, remove unnecessary services, apply vendor updates, and document accepted exceptions with an owner and review date. Verify the correction rather than assuming the ticket's closure changed the system."},
                {"heading": "A safe workflow", "body": "A responsible workflow is: obtain written authorization, define scope, notify stakeholders, run approved checks, minimize data collection, document evidence securely, report findings clearly, remediate, and retest. Findings should explain the affected asset, evidence, possible impact, and practical fix. Do not include unnecessary secrets in reports. If a test encounters unexpected sensitive data or an outage, stop according to the rules of engagement and notify the designated contact."},
